    "58381872a400502d9408"
   ],
   "duration": 39.202,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "So here's the shape of this whole topic: a lightweight encoding like run-length can win hugely, by real compression ratio, on data that's genuinely repetitive.",
     "start": 0.15,
     "end": 11.552
    },
    {
     "text": "That identical encoding genuinely loses outright on uniformly random data instead, in both its compression ratio and its own decode work, since there are simply no real runs there to collapse down.",
     "start": 11.972,
     "end": 26.4
    },
    {
     "text": "The real choice of which encoding to use has to follow the real shape of the actual data in front of it; it can never be applied by default, regardless of what that data actually looks like.",
     "start": 26.82,
     "end": 38.852
    }
   ],
